WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.152
Why is there a point inside every Gaussian splat?

00:00:03.152 --> 00:00:05.804
And what happens if that point becomes a line?

00:00:09.710 --> 00:00:10.693
Hi, I'm Mirabel!

00:00:10.693 --> 00:00:12.987
Five 3D reconstruction papers from this week.

00:00:12.987 --> 00:00:15.280
We'll go deep on two of them.

00:00:17.258 --> 00:00:17.799
Chapter one.

00:00:17.799 --> 00:00:19.152
A point was a choice.

00:00:24.258 --> 00:00:28.006
A three D Gaussian is a point, blurred by a smooth kernel.

00:00:28.006 --> 00:00:30.505
That makes it something a renderer can differentiate.

00:00:30.925 --> 00:00:34.346
So a splat has two knobs: the kernel, and the thing being blurred.

00:00:34.346 --> 00:00:37.241
Swap the point for a segment, or a curve, and you

00:00:37.241 --> 00:00:38.294
get these soft tubes.

00:00:39.558 --> 00:00:42.507
A Gaussian is a point blurred by a kernel.

00:00:42.507 --> 00:00:46.439
Spaghetti Splatting blurs a line segment instead, and stays in closed form.

00:00:49.258 --> 00:00:50.821
Take a segment, two endpoints.

00:00:50.821 --> 00:00:55.199
Every point along it adds a Gaussian, and together they make a soft tube.

00:00:55.199 --> 00:00:58.326
Set the endpoints equal and it is an ordinary splat.

00:00:58.746 --> 00:01:02.557
Along a camera ray, that tube integrates to two error functions.

00:01:02.557 --> 00:01:03.250
No sampling.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:08.006
Each segment gets an opacity per pixel, composited like ordinary splats.

00:01:08.426 --> 00:01:12.774
Cut a segment in half, and the two halves composite to exactly the parent.

00:01:12.774 --> 00:01:15.880
The image does not move, so training can split freely.

00:01:17.608 --> 00:01:18.732
Here is the formula.

00:01:19.608 --> 00:01:22.352
Find the point on the segment closest to the ray.

00:01:22.352 --> 00:01:25.371
One error function counts the part of the segment before it.

00:01:25.791 --> 00:01:27.818
The other counts the part after.

00:01:27.818 --> 00:01:32.210
A ray passing beyond an end drives one negative, and the contribution fades.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:35.359
A triangle would need a much harder function here.

00:01:35.359 --> 00:01:39.300
A segment needs only erf, which is why the authors stop at curves.

00:01:40.608 --> 00:01:43.829
With points, a thin strand needs a Gaussian every two kernel

00:01:43.829 --> 00:01:45.001
widths, or it beads.

00:01:45.421 --> 00:01:48.935
A segment draws it with one primitive, and bends with a simulator.

00:01:50.908 --> 00:01:54.258
The article's toy draws one line with Gaussian points.

00:01:54.258 --> 00:01:57.607
Five points, and the density ripples by seventeen percent.

00:01:58.027 --> 00:02:01.386
Six points, two kernel widths apart: two point nine percent.

00:02:01.806 --> 00:02:03.076
Seven: zero point three.

00:02:03.076 --> 00:02:05.934
A thin strand needs a point every two widths.

00:02:06.354 --> 00:02:10.626
One segment, convolved with the same kernel: one primitive, and no ripple at all.

00:02:11.046 --> 00:02:13.878
Cut it in half, slide the halves apart and back.

00:02:13.878 --> 00:02:17.277
The halves sum to the parent exactly, so the image does not

00:02:17.277 --> 00:02:18.976
move, and training can split freely.

00:02:20.758 --> 00:02:25.022
On a real room, the point version draws the bicycle spokes as scattered dots.

00:02:25.442 --> 00:02:28.883
The segments run along the spokes and edges, like pencil strokes.

00:02:30.508 --> 00:02:33.785
On rooms it ties ordinary splatting, as a superset should.

00:02:33.785 --> 00:02:38.374
On hair it wins outright, by two point seven decibels, with no hair prior.

00:02:39.758 --> 00:02:40.373
Chapter two.

00:02:40.373 --> 00:02:41.910
One scale is not enough.

00:02:47.008 --> 00:02:51.572
Splats reconstruct geometry badly, so people add a depth network as a prior.

00:02:51.572 --> 00:02:56.488
Its depth is relative, so it is aligned with one global scale and shift.

00:02:56.488 --> 00:02:58.946
That leaves forty-one percent of pixels off.

00:02:59.366 --> 00:03:02.760
Give each of three regions its own scale and shift, and eighty-four

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:03.892
point nine percent fit.

00:03:04.312 --> 00:03:06.016
With five, ninety-two point seven.

00:03:08.258 --> 00:03:10.131
The paper's scatter shows why.

00:03:10.131 --> 00:03:14.626
Predicted against true disparity falls on three different lines, one per surface.

00:03:15.046 --> 00:03:18.870
So AffineGS estimates a scale field that follows those surfaces.

00:03:20.608 --> 00:03:23.110
AffineGS models the relation per pixel.

00:03:23.530 --> 00:03:25.198
The network gives relative depth.

00:03:26.577 --> 00:03:30.309
The scale changes from surface to surface, not one number per image.

00:03:30.729 --> 00:03:33.674
A guided filter keeps both fields smooth within a surface.

00:03:33.674 --> 00:03:35.735
Without it they would copy the render.

00:03:37.008 --> 00:03:40.810
On a benchmark of indoor rooms, meshes score point seven one, against

00:03:40.810 --> 00:03:44.294
point two eight for a global fit the authors built themselves.

00:03:45.758 --> 00:03:46.531
Chapter three.

00:03:46.531 --> 00:03:47.690
Three more, briefly.

00:03:52.608 --> 00:03:54.419
Three more papers this week, briefly.

00:03:54.839 --> 00:03:58.288
Gaussian Grids hangs a feature grid on every 2D Gaussian.

00:03:58.708 --> 00:04:02.062
Another feeds a person's matching clicks into a point-map model.

00:04:02.482 --> 00:04:05.707
And one from Meta lets a stereo pair set metric depth,

00:04:05.707 --> 00:04:06.879
under a small decoder.

00:04:08.258 --> 00:04:09.176
Here is Click3R.

00:04:09.176 --> 00:04:12.848
A person clicks the same corner of a toaster in two photos.

00:04:13.268 --> 00:04:15.971
The base model alone splits the toaster in two.

00:04:15.971 --> 00:04:18.375
With one click, it is a single object.

00:04:20.108 --> 00:04:22.790
The article checked each claim against its own tables.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:26.692
Spaghetti's long connected strands average three and a half segments, and the

00:04:26.692 --> 00:04:28.143
code is not out yet.

00:04:28.563 --> 00:04:33.426
AffineGS initialises from sensor depth points, and tunes its filter scene by scene.

00:04:33.846 --> 00:04:37.370
And Gaussian Grids' point one bits per pixel is about point one

00:04:37.370 --> 00:04:39.132
three, by its own file sizes.

00:04:40.558 --> 00:04:43.338
Remember: the point inside a Gaussian was a choice.

00:04:43.338 --> 00:04:47.353
A segment keeps the closed form and draws thin things in one stroke.

00:04:49.608 --> 00:04:54.335
Segments instead of points, scale and shift per surface, and grids, clicks and stereo.

00:04:54.335 --> 00:04:55.348
Thanks for watching!

00:04:55.768 --> 00:04:57.904
Every source is in the full article.

00:04:57.904 --> 00:04:58.514
I'm Mirabel.

00:04:58.514 --> 00:04:58.819
Bye!
